{
  "bridge_text": "The wind carries many languages, but this ancient tree only hums in the tongue of the old world. With her hand pressed against the bark, Lina felt the vibration deep in her bones and stepped into the darkness.",
  "resume_strategy": "continue",
  "replacement_segments": [
    {
      "id": "s1",
      "text": "The vibration felt like a secret heartbeat, calling her closer. She pushed against the rough wood, and the bark swung open to reveal a hidden passage. Lina took a breath, stepped inside, and left the wool-grey village behind in the freezing night."
    },
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "text": "Inside the hollow tree she found a curl of stairs going down, and at the very top step sat an old grey fox in a patched green vest. His name was Mosk. He told her, in a voice like dry leaves, that the tree was not for visiting and that she should go home and forget the humming. Lina said she could not forget what she had not yet heard."
    }
  ],
  "active_scene_id": "s1"
}

{
  "bridge_text": "The fox’s grumpy heart hides a story that the shadows of the tree are just beginning to reveal. With the mystery still hanging in the air, Lina stood her ground on the dusty step.",
  "resume_strategy": "continue",
  "replacement_segments": [
    {
      "id": "s2",
      "text": "Lina stood her ground, her eyes meeting his weary gaze. She knew the secret of the humming was far too heavy to leave behind, and Mosk seemed to realize that his grumpy words were not enough to turn her back toward the woods."
    },
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "text": "Mosk pointed a shaky finger toward the door, but Lina reached into her pocket and pulled out the last apple of the season. She set it on the step between them, a small offering for the trouble of her visit. Mosk stared at the fruit for a long time until, finally, he sighed, stood up, and stepped onto the first stair without a word."
    }
  ],
  "active_scene_id": "s2"
}

{
  "bridge_text": "With Mosk’s name tucked safely in our pockets, we see him standing there, still guarding his secret. Lina holds her ground, hoping he might finally let her pass.",
  "resume_strategy": "skip",
  "replacement_segments": [
    {
      "id": "s3",
      "text": "Mosk pointed sternly at the exit, but Lina reached into her pocket and pulled out the last apple of the season. She set the crisp fruit on the step between them, offering it as a peace offering for her intrusion. The old fox stared at the apple for a long time, his weary eyes softening just a fraction. Without a word, he stood up, straightened his patched vest, and began to climb down the stairs."
    },
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "text": "At the bottom, the humming grew into a soft, sorrowful melody. A firefly the size of a robin lay curled on a bed of moss, her light flickering like a dying candle. Mosk’s whiskers trembled as he looked at her. He whispered that they were once dear friends, but one winter he had failed to bring her the medicine she needed. Ever since that day, he had kept watch, too ashamed to look her in the eye."
    }
  ],
  "active_scene_id": "s3"
}

{
