    }
  ],
  "active_scene_id": "s3"
}

{
  "bridge_text": "Just like that soft, velvet-green blanket of moss cushioned the firefly, the quiet of the hollow tree seemed to hold its breath for her. With the little light flickering, Mosk stepped closer to his old friend.",
  "resume_strategy": "restart",
  "replacement_segments": [
    {
      "id": "s4",
      "text": "At the bottom of the stairs, the humming was a quiet song. A firefly the size of a robin lay nestled on a bed of moss, that soft, velvet-green blanket for the earth. Her glow was almost out. Mosk’s whiskers trembled as he looked at her. She had been his friend once, he whispered, but one winter he had failed to bring her medicine in time. He had been guarding the tree ever since, too ashamed to face her."
    },
    {
      "id": "s5",
      "text": "Lina knelt and asked the firefly queen what she needed. A drop of warm milk and a kind word, said the queen, that was all medicine ever was. Mosk fetched the milk in a thimble and laid his paw beside hers. The queen's glow steadied, and the humming inside the tree turned into a tune Lina had not heard before. For the first time in years, Mosk hummed along."
    }
